Finding: {"title":"Parts stock quantity race condition — read-then-write with no transaction or row lock","severity":"HIGH","area":"Data Integrity / Concurrency","files":["src/server/routes/routes-parts.ts"],"detail":"`POST /parts/:id/transactions` (lines 594-694) reads the current `part` (qtyOnHand/qtyReserved), validates `part.qtyOnHand + signedQty >= 0` in application code, inserts a transaction row, then does a separate `UPDATE partsCatalog SET qtyOnHand = ...` using the value computed from the earlier read — all as unguarded, non-atomic, non-transactional statements (no `db.transaction()`, no `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, no atomic `qtyOnHand = qtyOnHand + $delta` SQL expression).","impact":"Two concurrent transactions against the same part (e.g. two mechanics scanning the same part on different work orders within the same second) can both read the same `qtyOnHand`, both pass the negative-stock check independently, and both write — one overwriting the other's update. This can drive `qtyOnHand` negative in the DB despite the application-level guard, or silently lose one of the two transactions' quantity effect while still recording both transaction ledger rows (so the ledger and the catalog quantity diverge).","recommendation":"Wrap the read-check-insert-update sequence in a DB transaction, and update `qtyOnHand`/`qtyReserved` using an atomic SQL expression (`SET qty_on_hand = qty_on_hand + ${signedQty}`) with a `WHERE qty_on_hand + ${signedQty} >= 0` guard so the negative-stock check is enforced atomically by the database rather than by a stale application-level read.","confidence":"high"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

